    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. i'm mostly worried about the scalability though. if we end up getting more manuscripts from the same era or region, which system is going to deal with the growing amount of metadata better without becoming a mess? lcc seems more complex, so is it harder to maintain as the collection expands?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "As your collection grows, LCC systems have proven scalable thanks to their comprehensive letter-number codes with intermediate subdivisions. Scalability is maintained by assigning alpha-characters for divisions, followed by a five-digit Cutter number that can introduce additional levels without necessitating a complete overhaul. While initially complex, LCC categorization enhances specialization as it grows. With proper training and consistent cataloging practices, LCC's complexity becomes an asset, not a liability. DDS may lead to larger, less detailed headings and could become cluttered with the addition of too many general subdivisions. In summary, LCC's layered structure provides flexibility and scalability for expanding rare manuscript collections.",
